WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
SPEAKER 1: Ideas never
translate automatically

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.080
into political action.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.360
They have to be
embodied in persons.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:17.250
By the late-1970s, the structure
of a conservative alternative

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.710
to elements of
Democratic orthodoxy

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.980
were clearly emerging
around certain principles

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.430
of smaller government
with lower taxes,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.510
a relatively free economy
with a minimum of regulation,

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.360
and opposition to communism.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.350
On all of these points and
more, the burgeoning movement

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.190
found a winning spokesman
in Ronald Reagan,

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.670
whose presidential
time had at last come

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.810
in 1980 after several
previous attempts.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.790
But Reagan was more
than just a vehicle

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.590
for other people's ideas.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.020
Ideas never translate
automatically

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.580
into political action.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.120
They have to be
embodied in persons.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.950
And Reagan's persona gave a
particular cast to the ideas

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.240
that he propounded.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.880
He was a superbly
gifted speaker who

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.130
had experience as a movie actor
and a television performer.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.890
He was entering into
a television age

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:17.820
he was the master of.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.050
HENRY TREWHITT: Mr.
President, I want

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.090
to raise an issue that I think
has been lurking out there

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.100
for two or three weeks
and cast it specifically

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.170
in national security terms.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.762
You already are the oldest
president in history,

00:01:27.762 --> 00:01:29.220
and some of your
staff say you were

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.530
tired after your most recent
encounter with Mr. Mondale.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.720
I recall yet that
President Kennedy

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.940
had to go for days on end
with very little sleep

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.560
during the Cuban Missile Crisis.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.050
Is there any doubt
in your mind that you

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.150
would be able to function
in such circumstances?

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.610
RONALD REAGAN: Not
at all, Mr. Trewhitt.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.270
And I want you to know that
also I will not make age

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.770
an issue of this campaign.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.060
I am not going to exploit
for political purposes

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.730
my opponent's youth
and inexperience.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.682
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:01.682 --> 00:02:03.634
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.220
SPEAKER 1: He had to run
against Jimmy Carter.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.540
Carter was no match for Reagan
in the 1980 campaign-- and not

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.460
only because Carter's
record in office

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:25.570
had been so poor and
so difficult to defend.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.700
Reagan offered a vision that
was more hopeful than Carter's.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.430
In his inauguration
address that day,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.480
Reagan blamed government
itself for many of the nation's

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.360
problems and proposed
that a more open,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.270
less heavily regulated,
less heavily taxed

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.610
economy would produce growth.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.400
The other principal
goal of Reagan

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.150
was to rebuild the nation's
standing in the world

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.260
after the humiliations
of Vietnam and Iran.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.320
And waging the Cold War
with the Soviet Union

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.420
required a more effective
and decisive American policy.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.530
As he said once, here's my
strategy on the Cold War--

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:12.040
we win and they lose.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.260
That view never changed,
although the details

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.260
filled themselves out
in different ways.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.500
The Soviet Union
was an evil empire.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.500
It was the focus of evil
in the modern world.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.250
He lambasted and
mocked communism

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:30.460
for its many failures, its
cruelty, its inefficiency,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.830
its disdain for human rights,
its incompetence, its atheism.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.170
He increased
spending on defense,

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:42.880
put into production a variety
of new defense systems--

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.300
the B-1 bomber, the
MX land-based missile.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.260
He built a 600-ship
American Navy.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.200
And most intriguing of
all, he started work

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.190
on what was called the Strategic
Defense Initiative, or SDI,

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.270
which critics called Star
Wars, because it used

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:05.950
lasers and particle beams
to shoot down incoming enemy

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:06.850
missiles.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.740
The critics said
it wouldn't work.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.350
And even if it did
work, it would only

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.460
fuel another round of the
arms race this time in space.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.810
The Euro Missile Crisis,
as it was called,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.810
was a turning point.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.029
And in the Soviet
Union, the ascent

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.820
of Mikhail Gorbachev
as Party Secretary,

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.790
General Secretary of
the Communist Party.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.980
There began a conspicuous change
in tone of Soviet leadership.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.710
Gorbachev wanted to
reform Soviet life.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.210
He wanted to open it up.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.490
He wanted to restructure it.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.430
Reagan grasped the
opportunities presented

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.540
by this change in power, worked
with Gorbachev favorably.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.710
Although he was insistent that
words be followed by deeds.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.300
When he spoke at the
Brandenburg Gate in West Berlin

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.085
at the Berlin Wall--

00:04:57.085 --> 00:05:00.250
RONALD REAGAN: General
Secretary Gorbachev,

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.560
if you seek peace, if you seek
prosperity for the Soviet Union

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.340
and Eastern Europe, if
you seek liberalization,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.740
come here to this gate.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.240
Mr. Gorbachev, open this gate.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.730
[CHEERING]

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:36.470
Mr. Gorbachev.

00:05:40.341 --> 00:05:44.950
Mr. Gorbachev, tear
down this wall.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:46.894
[CHEERING]

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.130
SPEAKER 1: Of course,
Gorbachev did not oblige him

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:56.490
in this regard, but he
did negotiate a treaty

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:01.290
to abolish intermediate
range nuclear missiles,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.660
first time the two
nations had agreed

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.360
to eliminate an entire
class of weapon,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.780
a move that would have been
unthinkable without Reagan's

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.830
firmness in the
Euro Missile Crisis.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.280
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.250
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.160
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.330
online courses.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:25.490
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

